WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.760
A dune plant was planted about sixty kilometres past the northern edge of its range.

2
00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.240
The planted population more than tripled.

3
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.000
If it can live there, why was it never there?

4
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.290
To answer, keep two things apart.

5
00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.890
One is the set of conditions a species can live in.

6
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.090
The other is the map of where it is found.

7
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.830
Draw the conditions first.

8
00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.290
Put temperature on one axis and soil moisture on the other.

9
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:39.350
Each sampled spot becomes a point, and one place can give several.

10
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:42.613
Now ask one question at each point.

11
00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:46.973
Count a local population once a year, with no newcomers.

12
00:00:48.413 --> 00:00:50.673
By what factor does it multiply that year?

13
00:00:52.073 --> 00:00:53.653
Ecologists call that factor lambda.

14
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:57.887
Years differ, so lambda changes from year to year.

15
00:00:59.107 --> 00:01:02.907
The test uses the long-run lambda, good and bad years together.

16
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:05.807
One good summer does not count.

17
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:11.887
Where long-run lambda is at least one, the population replaces itself without

18
00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:17.247
immigrants. That region of conditions is the niche, drawn as a teal box.

19
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.390
Now turn to the map.

20
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:25.250
Shade every stretch of coast whose conditions fall inside the box.

21
00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:29.070
That teal shading is where the species could live.

22
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.880
Then add the places where the species is actually found, as dark green dots.

23
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:37.900
The dots and the shading do not match.

24
00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:41.323
Picture a teal stretch with no dots.

25
00:01:42.563 --> 00:01:44.563
Its conditions are inside the niche.

26
00:01:45.823 --> 00:01:48.003
Nothing has arrived to start a population.

27
00:01:49.233 --> 00:01:52.473
So absence alone does not mark the edge of a niche.

28
00:01:53.673 --> 00:01:57.453
A species can be missing from a good place because it never got there.

29
00:01:58.467 --> 00:01:59.847
Now the other mismatch.

30
00:02:01.107 --> 00:02:04.867
A plant turns up at one site in every survey, year after year.

31
00:02:06.107 --> 00:02:07.687
Is that site inside its niche?

32
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.820
Not necessarily. Here deaths outnumber births, so lambda is below one.

33
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.840
Seeds arriving from a neighbouring site replace the dead every year.

34
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:29.623
Cut off the arriving seeds, and the plants dwindle away.

35
00:02:30.923 --> 00:02:34.723
A population like this, kept going by immigrants, is called a sink.

36
00:02:35.783 --> 00:02:38.743
So presence alone does not mark the niche either.

37
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:42.123
A map shows only where the species is found.

38
00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:46.543
Spotting sinks takes counts of births, deaths and immigrants.

39
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.787
Other species change lambda too.

40
00:02:51.027 --> 00:02:53.367
A competitor or an enemy can push it down.

41
00:02:54.627 --> 00:02:56.027
A helper can push it up.

42
00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.490
Back to the dune plant.

43
00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:02.250
Its planted population grew past the edge.

44
00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.710
So, at least at that site, the edge is not where the plant fails.

45
00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:12.003
A niche is where long-run lambda is at least one without immigrants.

46
00:03:13.363 --> 00:03:15.483
A map shows only where the species is found.
